{"name":"Artemisia","hex":"#e3ebea","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Artemisia","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A taxonomic genus within the family Asteraceae – artemisias and plants known as wormwood, sagebrush, and mugwort."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Artemisia"}],"entries":[{"title":"Artemisia I of Caria","blocks":[{"text":"Artemisia I of Caria was a queen of the ancient Greek city-state of Halicarnassus, which is now in Bodrum, Turkey. She was also queen of the nearby islands of Kos, Nisyros and Kalymnos, within the Achaemenid satrapy of Caria, in about 480 BC. She was of Carian-Greek ethnicity by her father Lygdamis I, and Greek Cretan by her mother. She fought as an ally of Xerxes I, King of Persia against the independent Greek city states during the second Persian invasion of Greece. She personally commanded ships at the naval battle of Artemisium and at the naval Battle of Salamis in 480 BC. She is mostly known through the writings of Herodotus, himself a native of Halicarnassus, who praises her courage and relates the respect in which she was held by Xerxes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Artemisia_at_the_Battle_of_Salamis.jpg/330px-Artemisia_at_the_Battle_of_Salamis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artemisia_I_of_Caria","wikidata":"Q232128"},{"title":"Artemisia II of Caria","blocks":[{"text":"Artemisia II of Caria was a naval strategist, commander and the sister and successor of Mausolus, ruler of Caria. Mausolus was a satrap of the Achaemenid Empire, yet enjoyed the status of king or dynast of the Hecatomnid dynasty. After the death of her brother/husband, Artemisia reigned for two years, from 353 to 351 BCE. Her ascension to the throne prompted a revolt in some of the island and coastal cities under her command due to their objection to a female ruler. Her administration was conducted on the same principles as that of her husband; in particular, she supported the oligarchical party on the island of Rhodes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Artemisia_II_original_and_reconstitution.jpg/330px-Artemisia_II_original_and_reconstitution.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artemisia_II_of_Caria","wikidata":"Q231851"},{"title":"Artemisia (plant)","blocks":[{"text":"Artemisia is a large, diverse genus of plants belonging to the daisy family, Asteraceae, with almost 500 species. Common names for various species in the genus include mugwort, wormwood, and sagebrush."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Artemisia_cina_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-165.jpg/330px-Artemisia_cina_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-165.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artemisia_(plant)","wikidata":"Q156421"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/CARIA_Circa_480-460_BC.jpg/500px-CARIA_Circa_480-460_BC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/CARIA_Circa_480-460_BC.jpg/500px-CARIA_Circa_480-460_BC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Coinage of Caria at the time of Artemisia (c. 480–460 BC).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACARIA_Circa_480-460_BC.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/CARIA%2C_Kaunos._Circa_470-450_BC.jpg/500px-CARIA%2C_Kaunos._Circa_470-450_BC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/CARIA%2C_Kaunos._Circa_470-450_BC.jpg/500px-CARIA%2C_Kaunos._Circa_470-450_BC.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org